S6.  Structural side: Paley-Wiener, and why the literal negative claim (B) is false
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THEOREM.  If g != 0 is compactly supported and |varphi(t)| <= C e^{-c|t|} on R for some
  c>0, then g = 0.  (Proof: g(u) = (1/2pi) int varphi(t) e^{-itu}dt converges absolutely
  and defines a holomorphic function in |Im u| < c; on the real axis it vanishes for
  |u| > A, an open set meeting the strip, so g = 0 by analytic continuation.)
  So a compactly supported kernel gives only algebraic / super-polynomial decay of
  varphi, never exponential decay.  The claim 'O(1) LHS forces a zero-side tail
  >= c/log G for every compactly supported even g' is therefore FALSE:
      compact bump A=12 m=40:  |LHS| = 2.0 ,  zero-tail bound at G = gamma_100 = 236.52423 is 4.95487e-77
      while 1/log G = 0.182947 -- the tail is many orders of magnitude below c/log G.
  Correct structural statement: if g is C^m and vanishes to order m at the endpoints of
  its support, then varphi(t) = O(t^{-(m+1)}) and the zero tail beyond G is O((log G)G^{-m}).
  Convergence needs m >= 1; m = 0 (a jump, e.g. the Cauchy kernel) is the divergent
  boundary case of truths S1.
  [ok] (B) refuted by the compact bump witness  -- tail 4.955e-77  <<  1/log G = 0.1829

